  "_comment": "How each biome looks beyond its props (Phase 9). Zones name a biome in their theme (content/zones/<id>.json, with the sky and fog colours); props take their per-biome art from tools/world_skins.py. Per biome: sun colour, energy and pitch (degrees below the horizon), ambient energy, fog density, an optional horizon colour; 'scatter' places walk-through ground clutter (a model in client/assets/env, per 100 square metres, a scale range, an optional #rrggbb tint); dungeon biomes give the ambient light colour and 'decor', models set along each room's walls (per room). Phase 11 surfaces: 'ground' names the outdoor ground's texture layers (client/assets/textures, from tools/gen_textures.py): base (the theme's ground colour, or base_tint), high (hilltops: the theme's high colour, or high_tint), slope and shore (near water), each a detail map on its tint; scale is metres per tile, skirt the height of the hills drawn past a zone's edge, shore_band how far above the water the shore layer reaches. 'sky': cloud cover 0..1, fog (how much fog veils the sky, 0..1), cloud and shade colours, sun_size. 'dungeon': floor, wall and wall_top textures with their scales and a roughness. Water itself is per zone (theme.water: level, color, opacity, sea sides). Client only: the server never reads this file.",
